We split $\ga$'s strategy into two main subcases: either (i) there is a corner
of $L_{U,49}$ that $\ga$ can reach in $98$ rounds without
being caught, or (ii) for every corner $v$ of $L_{U,49}$,
at least one of the following holds: (a) at least one of 
$\{\la_2,\la_3\}$ is at a distance of at most $98$
from $v$, or (b) $\la_1$ is at a distance of $97$ from $v$.
Each subcase is further broken into cases depending
on the relative initial positions of the cops.  The specific strategies used
by $\ga$ in each subcase are similar to those in Case (A) but the details are 
more tedious.
$\ga$'s strategy in the present case is summarised in
Algorithm \ref{algo:case2} (see Appendix  \ref{appendix:algo2} for the detailed analysis).  %The proof of the next lemma is deferred to Appendix \ref{appendix:algo2}.


%///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
% Algorithm for Case (B) 
