 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# One-cop-moves safe-spoke generator
     2	
     3	| Profile field | Value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     6	| Native domain | combinatorics |
     7	| Object regime | finite discrete |
     8	| Computational core | graph |
     9	| Certificate form | exact symbolic (a compressed canonical graph path) |
    10	| Intended intuition | change of variables |
    11	| Domain essentiality | native |
    12	| Reduction | none |
    13	
    14	This module turns Gao and Yang's [*The Cop Number of the One-Cop-Moves Game on Planar Graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.11184) into an unlimited family of exact escape-path problems.  The solver receives an implicit finite planar subdivided wheel, the positions of three cops, and a reversible labelling of its boundary.  It must name the one spoke whose canonical hub-to-boundary path is certified safe.  `verify` decodes that path and checks three exact graph-distance inequalities; it never reads the planted answer.
    15	
    16	The construction is native to the paper's objects.  Section 2 defines the alternating robber/one-active-cop game.  Section 5, Lemma 5.2 proves the executable certificate used here: a robber following a length-L path cannot be caught by a cop whose initial distance to the endpoint exceeds L.  This generator strengthens the sufficient check by requiring it separately for all three cops.  It samples the unique safe boundary position first, composes three closed cop-distance arcs around every other position, and only then relabels the graph.
    17	
    18	## Why Track B
    19	
    20	An efficient algorithm exists and is not hidden: decode the cyclic coordinates and exhaustively inspect the `2**n` endpoints.  This is `O(2**n)` for the implicit instance (ordinary BFS on the fully expanded graph would be much larger).  At the shipping preset, the measured reference run visited 8,418,459 endpoints, made 22,046,664 cop-distance tests, and used 132,280,080 accounted integer operations in 14.201239 seconds.  The eight-seed reference panel solved 8/8 in 94.569783 seconds and 1,076,068,680 operations.
    21	
    22	The compact route is 160 operations: undo the rotations, masks, bit permutation, and reflected-Gray encoding on three labels; view each cop's forbidden endpoints as a cyclic interval; find the sole gap; and encode it again.  That arithmetic is within the no-tool cap but is not mechanically replaceable by scanning 16,777,216 labels in context.  The paper is similarly explicit about the algorithmic side: Lemma 5.1 invokes Floyd–Warshall-style shortest-path reasoning, while Section 6 says the known full-game characterisations would require about `1.2e23` checks on the authors' 302,762-vertex graph.  The planar-separator upper bound in Section 6 concerns how many cops suffice, not a compact strategy for this position.
    23	
    24	## Worked demo
    25	
    26	The `demo` preset with seed 0 renders this complete instance:
    27	
    28	```text
    29	One-cop-moves escape certificate on a planar graph
    30	
    31	The finite undirected graph has a hub H and 16 spokes. A spoke is identified
    32	by an integer label x with 0 <= x < 16. For every label x it has vertices
    33	(x,1),...,(x,4); H is adjacent to (x,1), consecutive depths on one spoke are
    34	adjacent, and boundary vertices (x,4) form one cycle in the order below.
    35	
    36	Number cyclic positions i=0,...,15. All bit operations use 4 low bits, numbered
    37	from least-significant bit 0. A left rotation by r moves bit j to (j+r) mod 4.
    38	Let gray(z)=z XOR floor(z/2). To obtain B(i), start with gray((9+i) mod 16),
    39	move source bit j to output bit p[j] for p=[0,1,3,2], XOR with 12, and apply
    40	twists=[] from left to right. Boundary edges join B(i) to B((i+1) mod 16).
    41	
    42	The robber is at H and moves first. The cops are:
    43	  cop 1: (label=11, depth=2)
    44	  cop 2: (label=15, depth=2)
    45	  cop 3: (label=4, depth=2)
    46	
    47	Select x so the canonical path H,(x,1),...,(x,4) has endpoint distance
    48	strictly greater than 4 from every cop. Output exactly
    49	<answer>{"path":{"kind":"spoke","label":x}}</answer>.
    50	```
    51	
    52	The answer is `<answer>{"path":{"kind":"spoke","label":3}}</answer>`, and `verify` returns `(True, "ok")`.  Changing the label to 4 returns `(False, "cop 3 can reach the endpoint in 2 steps")`.  A person can solve this 16-spoke demo by listing the boundary order and crossing out the three cop arcs.
    53	
    54	## Difficulty presets
    55	
    56	| Preset | `n` | Encoding twists | Candidate spokes | Status |
    57	|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
    58	| demo | 4 | 0 | 16 | hand-solvable illustration |
    59	| easy | 20 | 1 | 1,048,576 | oracle unavailable |
    60	| medium | 22 | 2 | 4,194,304 | oracle unavailable |
    61	| hard | 24 | 3 | 16,777,216 | **shipping preset**, local gates pass |
    62	
    63	No preset was rejected by a local gate.  The required external ladder could not grade any preset because the configured OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” for every vendor draw.
    64	
    65	## Gate results
    66	
    67	| Gate | Result | Measurement |
    68	|---|---|---|
    69	| G1 | pass | 12/12 preset–seed plants verified |
    70	| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
    71	| G3 | pass | prose/fence/tag round-trip; garbage returns `None` |
    72	| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 hits; exact structure-aware density `1/16,777,216 = 5.96046e-8` |
    73	| G5 | pass | exactly 1 valid answer; baseline 14.201239 s, 8,418,459 endpoints, 132,280,080 operations |
    74	| G6 | pass | four attacks 0/8 each; reference algorithm 8/8 as expected |
    75	| G7 | pass | doubled `n=48` instance builds and verifies |
    76	| G8 | pass | 20/20 invariant relabellings, 20/20 carried witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
    77	| G9(c) | pass | 42 worst-case chars, 11 estimated tokens, 2 atoms, 160 intended operations |
    78	
    79	The four failing G6 attacks are label Hamming outlier, greedy largest numeric label gap, 256 random restarts, and the in-context XOR ansatz.  Plants and decoys are identically distributed under the random reversible labelling; every candidate boundary endpoint also has the same graph degree.
    80	
    81	## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostic
    82	
    83	| Run | Preset | Valid attempts | Solved | Outcome |
    84	|---|---|---:|---:|---|
    85	| bare | easy | 0 | 0 | four script-recorded HTTP 403 quota errors; no verdict |
    86	| structural hint | hard | 0 | 0 | four script-recorded HTTP 403 quota errors; no verdict |
    87	| placebo hint | hard | 0 | 0 | four script-recorded HTTP 403 quota errors; no verdict |
    88	
    89	Errors do not count as model failures, so `hinted - placebo` is unavailable—not zero evidence of an effect.  The three transcript files are retained exactly as written by `harden.py`.  Once quota is restored, all three runs must be repeated; until then there is no four-vendor hardness claim despite all local gates passing.  The structural hint only names the reflected-Gray cyclic order; it does not state the gap or a procedure.
    90	
    91	## Use
    92	
    93	From this result directory:
    94	
    95	```python
    96	import random
    97	import gen_1705_11184 as gen
    98	
    99	inst = gen.make_instance(seed=7, **gen.DIFFICULTY[gen.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
   100	prompt = gen.render(inst)
   101	answer = gen.parse_answer('<answer>{"path":{"kind":"spoke","label":0}}</answer>')
   102	ok, reason = gen.verify(inst, answer)
   103	candidate = gen.random_candidate(inst, random.Random(123))
   104	```
   105	
   106	From the repository root, emit instances with:
   107	
   108	```bash
   109	bash scripts/emit.sh 1705.11184 20
   110	```
   111	
   112	## Caveats
   113	
   114	This is a Lemma-5.2 path-certificate family, not a certificate of the full cop number of the authors' 302,762-vertex graph.  It tests recognizing a hidden cyclic coordinate; providing decoded boundary ranks, removing the label scrambling, or using a script makes it easy.  G4 samples uniformly from every correctly shaped spoke descriptor—the exact prior implied by the statement—and proves resistance to unstructured guessing, but it says nothing about a solver that notices the Gray change of variables.  The adversary panel did not test learned Gray-code recognition, symbolic bit-vector solvers, SAT/SMT encodings, or provider models because the external quota was exhausted.  The canonical key completely handles the representable dihedral, arbitrary bit-permutation/rotate-XOR label, and cop-order symmetries; it does not attempt arbitrary coloured-graph isomorphism.  The module remains standard-library-only if `gvlib` is absent.

